1
00:00:00,697 --> 00:00:03,566
Hi, everyone, and thanks for joining.

2
00:00:03,997 --> 00:00:05,107
I am Pavel Konnikov.

3
00:00:05,412 --> 00:00:09,892
I lead IT development and the
cloud platform at Novacard, a

4
00:00:09,906 --> 00:00:12,273
fast-growing fintech on AWS.

5
00:00:13,076 --> 00:00:15,691
Before that, I co-founded BinoData.

6
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:19,780
We built executable disaster
recovery plans for the cloud.

7
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:24,984
Today, I want to talk about
one gap in chaos engineering.

8
00:00:25,586 --> 00:00:30,858
We are really good at testing the
loss of one data center, but in

9
00:00:30,858 --> 00:00:36,634
the last two years, cloud regions
were lost in different way, and our

10
00:00:36,661 --> 00:00:39,261
experiments didn't prepare us for it.

11
00:00:40,297 --> 00:00:42,391
Let me start with my own mistake.

12
00:00:42,754 --> 00:00:46,452
Around 2020, I was at
Alfa Bank Kazakhstan.

13
00:00:47,556 --> 00:00:50,974
One day, our core
network equipment failed.

14
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:53,029
We knew it was old.

15
00:00:53,273 --> 00:00:54,961
We had planned to replace it.

16
00:00:55,126 --> 00:01:01,254
But procurement was slow,
and the chip shortage hit.

17
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:07,909
So when it broke, there was
nothing in the warehouse.

18
00:01:08,496 --> 00:01:12,031
For a whole day, customers
couldn't use the bank remotely.

19
00:01:12,586 --> 00:01:13,816
The data was fine.

20
00:01:14,091 --> 00:01:15,385
The systems were running.

21
00:01:15,597 --> 00:01:17,166
We just couldn't reach them.

22
00:01:18,192 --> 00:01:20,168
And yes, we had a recovery plan.

23
00:01:20,694 --> 00:01:26,056
One paper We just had
nothing to run it with.

24
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:30,128
A plan you can't execute is not a plan.

25
00:01:30,464 --> 00:01:31,545
Keep that in mind.

26
00:01:32,348 --> 00:01:35,259
It's what this talk is about.

27
00:01:36,198 --> 00:01:39,137
On the left, what most of us test.

28
00:01:39,491 --> 00:01:43,733
One zone goes down,
traffic moves, we survive.

29
00:01:44,351 --> 00:01:49,595
On the right, what actually happened in
twenty twenty-five and twenty twenty-six.

30
00:01:50,356 --> 00:01:58,728
One data center lost, then another
hours or week later, and the cloud's own

31
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,698
management layer failing at the same time.

32
00:02:02,573 --> 00:02:04,309
That is different experiment.

33
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:07,561
Most of us have never run it.

34
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:12,373
To be fair, the industry has done a lot.

35
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:18,102
Since twenty twenty-three, AWS
fault injection service can simulate

36
00:02:18,147 --> 00:02:20,535
a full power cut in one zone.

37
00:02:21,216 --> 00:02:28,072
Since August this year, Resilience Hub
has test for losing a zone, a region,

38
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:33,399
or a dependency, and Netflix has been
evacuating whole regions for years.

39
00:02:33,688 --> 00:02:39,920
By twenty eighteen, their Chaos
Kong drills run every few weeks

40
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:41,976
and took under ten minutes.

41
00:02:42,742 --> 00:02:45,412
So the tools are h- there.

42
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:51,172
If your team survives losing
one zone, that is real progress.

43
00:02:51,591 --> 00:02:52,509
Be proud of it.

44
00:02:53,218 --> 00:03:00,680
But they all assume one thing,
the failure happens once, and the

45
00:03:00,682 --> 00:03:02,881
rest of the cloud keeps working.

46
00:03:03,881 --> 00:03:06,132
Here is what happened instead.

47
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:13,264
March twenty twenty-six, AWS in
the UAE, one zone was lost after

48
00:03:13,352 --> 00:03:16,461
a physical strike on the facility.

49
00:03:17,318 --> 00:03:20,517
About seventeen hours
later, a second zone.

50
00:03:21,241 --> 00:03:26,319
In Bahrain, the second zone
went one or two months later.

51
00:03:27,486 --> 00:03:32,486
October twenty twenty-six, Yandex
Cloud, their data-- three data

52
00:03:32,486 --> 00:03:38,822
centers is in about seventy-five
hours, day one, day two, day four,

53
00:03:39,896 --> 00:03:43,048
and it's not only about attacks.

54
00:03:43,527 --> 00:03:48,728
In October twenty twenty-five,
a bug in DynamoDB, DNS

55
00:03:48,928 --> 00:03:52,334
automation hit US East One.

56
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:57,669
For about fourteen hours, you
couldn't launch new EC2 instances.

57
00:03:58,604 --> 00:04:00,575
Now, look at the gaps.

58
00:04:01,214 --> 00:04:04,549
Seventeen hours, three days, two months.

59
00:04:06,114 --> 00:04:14,594
That's the window your team has to act
Have you ever practiced acting in it?

60
00:04:15,721 --> 00:04:20,982
When you read the status updates
hour by hour, you see a pattern.

61
00:04:21,285 --> 00:04:24,060
Each stage breaks something different.

62
00:04:24,695 --> 00:04:31,730
In the UAE, after the first zone,
elastic IPs were stuck in the dead

63
00:04:31,732 --> 00:04:34,497
zone for about thirteen hours.

64
00:04:35,128 --> 00:04:39,864
After the second zone, you couldn't
launch anything anywhere in the region,

65
00:04:40,869 --> 00:04:45,386
even in the zone that was still healthy.

66
00:04:46,378 --> 00:04:51,375
S3 was built to survive one zone, not two.

67
00:04:52,598 --> 00:04:58,710
At Yandex, three and half hours
in limits appeared on new virtual

68
00:04:58,711 --> 00:05:03,607
machines, Kubernetes clusters,
and databases in the live zones.

69
00:05:04,203 --> 00:05:10,167
For about thirty-eight hours, you
couldn't move Kubernetes masters

70
00:05:10,177 --> 00:05:12,777
or database out of the dead zone.

71
00:05:13,948 --> 00:05:16,076
Object storage was read-only.

72
00:05:16,399 --> 00:05:19,776
Database failover only through support.

73
00:05:21,708 --> 00:05:29,327
So the healthy zones was alive You
just couldn't change anything in it.

74
00:05:30,431 --> 00:05:34,288
A one-zone experiment
never shows you that.

75
00:05:35,671 --> 00:05:39,565
You might think, "That is war.

76
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:41,414
My region is safe."

77
00:05:41,873 --> 00:05:45,901
So here is an example
with no attack at all.

78
00:05:46,632 --> 00:05:53,521
October 2026, Google Cloud, London, one
zone lost power for about six hours.

79
00:05:54,444 --> 00:05:56,885
One power feed was in maintenance.

80
00:05:57,383 --> 00:06:01,205
The other one failed, and
the generators didn't start.

81
00:06:02,112 --> 00:06:04,000
Only one zone went dark.

82
00:06:04,799 --> 00:06:08,679
But the other two zones degraded, too.

83
00:06:09,458 --> 00:06:12,308
Some services called into
the dead zone synchronously.

84
00:06:13,647 --> 00:06:20,161
One service had two of its three
repl- replicas in that zone, and

85
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:23,714
there is-- wasn't enough capacity
for everyone failing over.

86
00:06:25,476 --> 00:06:31,941
And in us-east-1 in 2025, the
load balancer failover, a safety

87
00:06:31,941 --> 00:06:34,606
feature, made things worse.

88
00:06:34,838 --> 00:06:44,330
Engineers switched it off by hand for four
and half hours, so zones are not in…

89
00:06:44,505 --> 00:06:46,430
as in-independent as we think.

90
00:06:47,430 --> 00:06:52,005
From these post-mortems,
I've got seven failure modes.

91
00:06:52,839 --> 00:06:56,880
I think most chaos
programs test none of them.

92
00:06:58,152 --> 00:07:01,842
One, the control plane breaks first.

93
00:07:02,532 --> 00:07:06,630
Running things run, but nothing
new can be created or moved.

94
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:15,921
Two, hidden dependencies between zones
and on S3, DynamoDB, IAM, and DNS.

95
00:07:16,656 --> 00:07:18,126
Three, quorums.

96
00:07:18,897 --> 00:07:22,950
You survive the first
loss on two of three.

97
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:26,190
The second one kills you.

98
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:30,834
Four, data tied to a region.

99
00:07:31,577 --> 00:07:35,411
The backup survives, but you
can't restore it anywhere.

100
00:07:36,582 --> 00:07:38,200
Five, capacity.

101
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:42,346
It turns out for everyone
at the same time.

102
00:07:43,493 --> 00:07:49,660
Six, automation and health checks
that makes the outage bigger.

103
00:07:50,914 --> 00:07:53,551
Seven, the status page is late.

104
00:07:54,227 --> 00:07:58,701
Five hours in the UAE,
two and half in London.

105
00:08:00,651 --> 00:08:02,338
So why now?

106
00:08:03,596 --> 00:08:06,124
Because the probabilities have flipped.

107
00:08:07,065 --> 00:08:09,928
Zones sit many kilometers apart.

108
00:08:10,620 --> 00:08:15,504
If they fail independently,
losing all three is P cubed.

109
00:08:16,392 --> 00:08:21,873
With one percent a year, that is
one in a million, basically never.

110
00:08:22,819 --> 00:08:28,905
But reliability engineers have known
for years that is too optimistic.

111
00:08:30,301 --> 00:08:35,212
There is always a common cause
that hits everything at once.

112
00:08:35,854 --> 00:08:43,361
So it's P cubed plus beta times
P, and the second part wins.

113
00:08:44,978 --> 00:08:48,611
These numbers are just an
illustration, not a forecast.

114
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:51,786
Independent zones, one in a million.

115
00:08:52,558 --> 00:08:58,540
Add a rare natural event and a
small chance of attack, about zero

116
00:08:58,542 --> 00:09:01,930
point four percent a year, a year.

117
00:09:03,644 --> 00:09:06,351
In a tense year, about three percent.

118
00:09:07,847 --> 00:09:10,861
That is once in thirty years, not never.

119
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,838
And an attack is worse than any model.

120
00:09:16,148 --> 00:09:17,561
It's sequential.

121
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:22,556
The zone you moved to is the next target.

122
00:09:23,955 --> 00:09:30,889
Distance protects you from nature, not
from someone aiming at every data center.

123
00:09:32,354 --> 00:09:37,282
So the one zone experiment
answers the questions.

124
00:09:38,049 --> 00:09:40,604
That is no longer the main one.

125
00:09:41,826 --> 00:09:47,512
So how do you rehearse losing a
region without losing production?

126
00:09:48,585 --> 00:09:49,821
Here is my answer.

127
00:09:50,953 --> 00:09:55,232
Design your game days for the region.

128
00:09:55,848 --> 00:09:58,309
Inject failures at the zone.

129
00:09:59,294 --> 00:10:04,401
Then step by step, take away
that real incidents took away.

130
00:10:05,391 --> 00:10:12,185
The second zone, the control plane, the
backup, the capacity, the status page,

131
00:10:12,692 --> 00:10:20,139
and measure how long it takes to decide,
not just how long it takes to fail over.

132
00:10:21,139 --> 00:10:29,608
Four parts: hypothesis, injection,
a ladder of game days, and metrics.

133
00:10:31,409 --> 00:10:37,984
Chaos engineering starts with a
steady state and hypothesis that

134
00:10:38,004 --> 00:10:44,428
doesn't change here Your steady
state should be a business metric.

135
00:10:45,409 --> 00:10:50,431
Payments per minute,
logins, orders, not CPU.

136
00:10:51,954 --> 00:10:56,735
Then write a hypothesis
that can fail like this.

137
00:10:57,729 --> 00:11:05,683
Given zone A is lost and the control
plane is read-only, we can still

138
00:11:05,851 --> 00:11:11,656
shift traffic and keep payments above
ninety-five percent within 15 minutes.

139
00:11:12,714 --> 00:11:17,718
It names the failure, the
real-world constraint, the

140
00:11:17,718 --> 00:11:19,706
business metric, and the time.

141
00:11:20,629 --> 00:11:25,919
Drop any of them, and the test
passes and teaches you nothing.

142
00:11:27,276 --> 00:11:33,270
Here, each failure mode becomes a
hypothesis and a way to inject it.

143
00:11:34,254 --> 00:11:38,706
Control plane gone, we recover
without creating anything new.

144
00:11:39,689 --> 00:11:44,843
Hidden dependencies, we stay
up without one core service.

145
00:11:45,824 --> 00:11:50,286
Quorum, we move the third
replica before the second loss.

146
00:11:51,357 --> 00:11:55,047
Data, we restore from a
copy outside the provider.

147
00:11:56,154 --> 00:12:00,678
Capacity, what we reserved is enough.

148
00:12:01,897 --> 00:12:06,210
Automation, one person
can stop it in one step.

149
00:12:07,308 --> 00:12:11,608
Status page, we declare a
disaster from our own signals.

150
00:12:12,677 --> 00:12:15,649
You won't test all seven next week.

151
00:12:16,369 --> 00:12:19,614
Pick the one that scares you most.

152
00:12:20,210 --> 00:12:22,059
It's usually the right one.

153
00:12:23,318 --> 00:12:29,494
The question I hear most, "How
do I simulate a provider failure?

154
00:12:30,101 --> 00:12:31,721
I don't control the provider."

155
00:12:32,847 --> 00:12:34,203
You don't need to.

156
00:12:34,936 --> 00:12:37,813
You simulate what it
looks like from your side.

157
00:12:38,943 --> 00:12:42,029
To break the control plane, deny it.

158
00:12:42,611 --> 00:12:48,740
On AWS, a service control policy or IAM
policy can block creating instances,

159
00:12:49,064 --> 00:12:52,041
changing DNS, or changing load balancers.

160
00:12:52,684 --> 00:12:57,985
Everything that is already running
keeps working, just like the data

161
00:12:57,986 --> 00:13:04,591
plane in a real event, and your
automation, your pipelines, and

162
00:13:04,604 --> 00:13:08,568
your engineers hit access denied.

163
00:13:10,072 --> 00:13:12,559
What you find is usually painful.

164
00:13:13,153 --> 00:13:16,300
Your recovery needs new resources.

165
00:13:16,780 --> 00:13:20,064
Your pipelines live in the same region.

166
00:13:20,352 --> 00:13:27,133
Your secret, your Terraform state,
your login, all depend on the

167
00:13:27,135 --> 00:13:33,951
region you are trying to leave
The fix is static stability.

168
00:13:34,776 --> 00:13:40,015
Everything you need to recover
is already there, and you

169
00:13:40,015 --> 00:13:42,326
switch it to the data plane.

170
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:46,365
Same idea for backups.

171
00:13:47,050 --> 00:13:52,098
Most teams restore in the same
account, in the same region.

172
00:13:52,547 --> 00:13:54,931
That proves the backup exists.

173
00:13:56,057 --> 00:14:00,202
It doesn't prove you
can use it on a bad day.

174
00:14:01,195 --> 00:14:05,770
So in the game day, block
access to your main backups.

175
00:14:06,291 --> 00:14:13,061
Now, the team has to restore from the
outside copy with separate credentials

176
00:14:13,190 --> 00:14:15,269
into another region or cloud.

177
00:14:16,473 --> 00:14:23,960
For capacity, lower your quotas to what
you actually reserved, and for the status

178
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:27,465
page, the facilitator just hides it.

179
00:14:28,386 --> 00:14:33,683
The team gets only its own
monitoring and outside signals.

180
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:37,667
That is what the first
hours really feel like.

181
00:14:38,961 --> 00:14:41,328
None of this starts in production.

182
00:14:42,527 --> 00:14:45,062
Grow the blast radius step by step.

183
00:14:45,753 --> 00:14:51,441
First, a table top on paper, then
staging, then a small slice of

184
00:14:51,461 --> 00:14:58,441
production traffic, and only then a full
region evacuation, like Netflix did.

185
00:14:59,523 --> 00:15:04,464
The rule: move on only when
the last step got boring.

186
00:15:04,807 --> 00:15:08,794
No surprises, no heroes, no improvising.

187
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:14,605
And agree your stop
conditions before you start.

188
00:15:15,222 --> 00:15:18,295
The business metric drops too low, stop.

189
00:15:18,749 --> 00:15:21,842
Real customer data at risk, stop.

190
00:15:22,281 --> 00:15:25,693
And one person always has a stop button.

191
00:15:26,934 --> 00:15:29,762
Here is the ladder I had suggest.

192
00:15:30,408 --> 00:15:34,536
Six game days, each one
removes one more assumption.

193
00:15:35,492 --> 00:15:37,641
Level zero, a tabletop.

194
00:15:38,265 --> 00:15:45,723
Read the UIE timeline hour by
hour and ask, "What do we do now?"

195
00:15:46,810 --> 00:15:49,518
Level one, lose one zone.

196
00:15:49,965 --> 00:15:52,282
Most of you already do this.

197
00:15:53,345 --> 00:15:58,139
Level two, lose a second
zone a few hours later.

198
00:15:58,718 --> 00:16:01,862
Now you test quorums and capacity.

199
00:16:02,815 --> 00:16:07,131
Level three, lose a zone
with no control plane.

200
00:16:07,899 --> 00:16:10,177
Is your recovery statically stable?

201
00:16:11,177 --> 00:16:15,578
Level four, restore from a
copy outside the provider.

202
00:16:16,096 --> 00:16:17,959
That is your real RPO.

203
00:16:18,990 --> 00:16:24,318
Level five, evacuate the region
on a human decision with a

204
00:16:24,387 --> 00:16:26,280
timer from the first signal.

205
00:16:27,481 --> 00:16:33,309
You don't need to finish the ladder this
quarter, just know which step you are on.

206
00:16:34,309 --> 00:16:36,906
Let me zoom in on level two.

207
00:16:37,887 --> 00:16:40,814
It surprises people the most.

208
00:16:41,627 --> 00:16:46,429
A typical database cluster has
three replicas in three zones.

209
00:16:47,134 --> 00:16:49,532
It survives losing one.

210
00:16:50,398 --> 00:16:53,098
Now it runs on two of three.

211
00:16:54,262 --> 00:16:57,194
It still works, so everyone relaxes.

212
00:16:58,389 --> 00:17:01,789
But there is no margin left.

213
00:17:02,212 --> 00:17:11,714
The second loss means one of three and
writes stop We saw this in Google's Paris

214
00:17:11,747 --> 00:17:14,333
region in two thousand and twenty-three.

215
00:17:15,299 --> 00:17:20,521
Two of three replicas of a regional
database were in the affected

216
00:17:20,521 --> 00:17:26,811
location, and in London this year,
two of three replicas in one zone.

217
00:17:27,978 --> 00:17:35,850
So the level two hypothesis is after
the first loss, we get back to three

218
00:17:35,976 --> 00:17:39,490
healthy replicas before the second one.

219
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:44,275
In the UAE, that window
was about seventeen hours.

220
00:17:45,365 --> 00:17:50,977
Your game day will tell you if
that is enough for your team.

221
00:17:52,724 --> 00:17:54,851
So what do you measure?

222
00:17:55,841 --> 00:17:57,103
Six numbers.

223
00:17:58,311 --> 00:18:03,041
Time to detect from the first
signal until someone knows.

224
00:18:03,906 --> 00:18:09,261
Time to decide until someone
says, "This is a disaster.

225
00:18:09,728 --> 00:18:10,831
We are leaving."

226
00:18:11,901 --> 00:18:15,638
In real events, that is
often the longest part.

227
00:18:16,304 --> 00:18:19,148
Everyone waits for a status page.

228
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:25,411
Real RTO, until the
business metrics is back.

229
00:18:26,427 --> 00:18:31,942
Real RPO, the data you actually
lost, not the number in the document.

230
00:18:33,061 --> 00:18:34,639
Steps from the plan.

231
00:18:35,188 --> 00:18:39,790
How much went by the plan
and how much was improvised?

232
00:18:40,447 --> 00:18:44,038
And hidden dependencies,
how many you found.

233
00:18:45,291 --> 00:18:48,114
The last two are my favorites.

234
00:18:48,743 --> 00:18:52,495
They show how real your plan is.

235
00:18:53,327 --> 00:18:54,824
Why does this matter?

236
00:18:55,473 --> 00:18:59,021
Because most teams don't
know these numbers.

237
00:19:00,024 --> 00:19:06,439
In VMware's two thousand and
twenty-four report, only thirteen

238
00:19:06,475 --> 00:19:12,552
percent said they could really
orchestrate recovery in a disaster.

239
00:19:13,563 --> 00:19:15,666
I saw the same at BinoData.

240
00:19:16,203 --> 00:19:22,235
Most teams we worked with had never
run a recovery drill, and when

241
00:19:22,246 --> 00:19:27,705
they ran the first one, the restore
usually didn't go as planned.

242
00:19:28,755 --> 00:19:32,731
That is why we built executable
disaster recovery plans.

243
00:19:33,448 --> 00:19:41,283
A plan that runs as a code can be
rehearsed every month, not once a year.

244
00:19:42,423 --> 00:19:45,188
A plan you can't execute is not a plan.

245
00:19:45,916 --> 00:19:49,013
A plan you have never executed is a hope.

246
00:19:50,698 --> 00:19:53,882
So what can you do next sprint?

247
00:19:54,828 --> 00:19:55,689
Three things.

248
00:19:56,409 --> 00:19:57,506
One tabletop.

249
00:19:57,949 --> 00:20:05,956
Take the UAE or the Yandex timeline and
walk your team through it hour by hour.

250
00:20:07,947 --> 00:20:10,069
Two hours, no infrastructure.

251
00:20:11,469 --> 00:20:13,096
One hypothesis.

252
00:20:13,857 --> 00:20:17,009
Pick the failure that scares you most.

253
00:20:17,158 --> 00:20:20,392
Write it down with a
business metric and a time.

254
00:20:21,051 --> 00:20:28,143
Run it in staging One number
measure your time to decide.

255
00:20:28,979 --> 00:20:29,747
Just that.

256
00:20:30,207 --> 00:20:31,666
You will be surprised

257
00:20:32,666 --> 00:20:33,506
One number.

258
00:20:34,384 --> 00:20:37,878
Measure your time to decide just that.

259
00:20:38,561 --> 00:20:40,005
You will be surprised.

260
00:20:40,787 --> 00:20:48,200
And one thing for the leaders watching,
a regional game day isn't a SRE hobby.

261
00:20:49,670 --> 00:20:53,927
It needs time, money, and
permission to touch production.

262
00:20:54,601 --> 00:20:56,708
That is a leadership decision.

263
00:20:57,840 --> 00:21:02,353
When a company isn't ready to
lose a region, it's usually

264
00:21:02,601 --> 00:21:04,056
not the engineer's failure.

265
00:21:04,817 --> 00:21:06,520
It's a leadership failure.

266
00:21:07,995 --> 00:21:10,165
The probabilities have flipped.

267
00:21:10,859 --> 00:21:15,011
Regions are now lost one
data center at a time.

268
00:21:15,576 --> 00:21:20,489
Let's design our chaos
experiments the same way.

269
00:21:22,085 --> 00:21:23,191
Thanks for listening.

270
00:21:23,713 --> 00:21:26,335
Find me on LinkedIn, Pavel Konnikov.

271
00:21:26,746 --> 00:21:31,542
I would love to hear how your
team runs regional game days

272
00:21:31,910 --> 00:21:34,818
and what broke when you tried.

